[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Data Types: Primitive vs. Reference"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript, like many programming languages, categorizes data into different types. Understanding these types is crucial for writing efficient and bug-free code. The two main categories are primitive data types and reference data types."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Primitive Data Types"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Primitive data types are immutable, meaning their values cannot be changed after they are created. When you assign a primitive value to a variable, the variable holds the actual value itself. JavaScript has seven primitive data types:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Number: Represents numeric values, including integers and floating-point numbers (e.g., 10, 3.14)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"String: Represents textual data (e.g., "Hello, world!")."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Boolean: Represents logical values, either true or false."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Null: Represents the intentional absence of a value. It has only one value: null."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Undefined: Represents a variable that has been declared but has not been assigned a value."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Symbol (ES6): Represents a unique and immutable identifier."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BigInt (ES2020): Represents integers of arbitrary precision."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Examples of primitive data types
let age = 30; // Number
let name = 'John Doe'; // String
let isStudent = false; // Boolean
let emptyValue = null; // Null
let notAssigned; // Undefined
const uniqueID = Symbol('id'); // Symbol
const bigNumber = 123456789012345678901234567890n; // BigInt"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Reference Data Types"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reference data types are mutable, meaning their values can be changed after they are created. When you assign a reference value to a variable, the variable holds a reference (or pointer) to the memory location where the object is stored, not the actual value itself. The primary reference data type in JavaScript is the object. Arrays and functions are special types of objects."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Object: A collection of key-value pairs (e.g., { name: "Alice", age: 25 })."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Array: An ordered list of values (e.g., [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Function: A block of code designed to perform a particular task."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// Examples of reference data types
let person = { name: "Alice", age: 30 }; // Object
let numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]; // Array
function greet(name) { // Function
  console.log('Hello, ' + name + '!');
}
"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences: Immutability and Pass-by-Value vs. Pass-by-Reference"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The core difference lies in how values are stored and copied. Primitive types are immutable and passed by value, while reference types are mutable and passed by reference."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pass-by-Value: When you assign a primitive value to a new variable, a copy of the value is created. Changes to the new variable do not affect the original variable."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"let x = 10;
let y = x; // y gets a copy of x's value (10)
y = 20; // Changing y does not affect x
console.log(x); // Output: 10
console.log(y); // Output: 20"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pass-by-Reference: When you assign a reference value to a new variable, a copy of the reference (pointer) is created. Both variables now point to the same object in memory. Changes made through one variable will affect the other."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"let obj1 = { value: 10 };
let obj2 = obj1; // obj2 gets a reference to the same object as obj1
obj2.value = 20; // Changing obj2.value also changes obj1.value
console.log(obj1.value); // Output: 20
console.log(obj2.value); // Output: 20"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Implications and Considerations"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the difference between primitive and reference types is crucial for avoiding unexpected behavior in your JavaScript code. Be mindful of how you are manipulating data, especially when working with objects and arrays. If you need to modify a reference type without affecting the original, create a copy of the object or array using methods like 'Object.assign()' or the spread operator ('...')."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"// Creating a copy of an object using Object.assign()
let originalObj = { a: 1, b: 2 };
let copiedObj = Object.assign({}, originalObj);
copiedObj.a = 3; // Modifying copiedObj does not affect originalObj
console.log(originalObj); // Output: { a: 1, b: 2 }
console.log(copiedObj); // Output: { a: 3, b: 2 }

// Creating a copy of an array using the spread operator
let originalArray = [1, 2, 3];
let copiedArray = [...originalArray];
copiedArray[0] = 4; // Modifying copiedArray does not affect originalArray
console.log(originalArray); // Output: [1, 2, 3]
console.log(copiedArray); // Output: [4, 2, 3]"}]
